  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Engineers are testing a water pump filling a reservoir.\nThey observe that water enters the reservoir at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=12\\sqrt{t+4},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in gallons per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the pump was turned on.\nAt the same time, water leaks from the reservoir at a constant rate of $6$ gallons per minute.\n\nIf the reservoir was initially empty, find the expression that gives $V(t),$ the volume of water in the reservoir $t$ minutes after the pump was turned on.\n\n$V(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-\\,6t-64$",
  "worked_solution": "Water is entering the reservoir at a rate of $r(t)$ gallons per minute and is leaking at a rate of $6$ gallons per minute. Therefore, the **rate of change** $R(t)$ of the volume of water is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-6 \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{t+4}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the total volume $V(t)$ after $t$ minutes, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}(12\\sqrt{x+4}-6)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. Also, since the reservoir is initially empty, we set $V(0)=0.$\n\nWe can simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}12\\sqrt{x+4}\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{t}6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={12\\cdot \\frac{2(x+4)^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{t}-6x{|}_{0}^{t} \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-8(4)^{3/2}-6t \\\\  & =8(t+4)^{3/2}-6t-64\\end{aligned}\n$$",
